            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "TaraKD"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Absolutely delicious! I made several different nut recipes today, and this is the one I can't stop eating. The only change I made was to use Splenda instead of sugar. This is so good!!! It's just slightly sweet and slightly spicy, which is a refreshing change from most recipes for nuts that are incredibly sweet or way too spicy -- plus, it's an interesting taste to have both in one recipe."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CaringFlan7802"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great recipe but required some minor changes. Cut the salt in half. Mixed the sugar, salt and cayenne into the nuts in the skilled at medium temp and continue to stir until the sugar is melted onto the almonds, being careful not to burn them.",
            "datePublished": "2023-04-17T21:59:29.545Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Kathy"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Made these almonds for a Christmas snack but made two small changes. I added just a dusting of cinnamon to the dry ingredients. After coating the nuts with the sugar mixture, I places them back into the 350 degree oven for about 5 minutes until the sugar melted and coated the nuts (careful not to over bake them as they burn quickly). This made them much neater to eat (no sugar on your fingers) and gave them additional crunch. They were a bit hit. Even better the next day! I'll definitely make these again.",
            "datePublished": "2018-12-26T19:49:54.930Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Allrecipes Member"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I doubled the recipe, added the hot almonds right out of the oven into the saucepan, mixed together until all liquid was absorbed then added them to the sugar/spice mixture ( I added cinnamon). I let them cool to room temp. No problem. Wonderful recipe.",
            "datePublished": "2013-12-22T17:54:16.600Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jennifer S"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I've been making this recipe for years. So easy and delicious. My go to \"take along\" to holiday parties. At least 15 people have asked me for the recipe. I always double the recipe and use equal amounts of both pecans and almonds. The only other change I make is that the original recipe is waaay too salty. I cut back the salt by 1/2 tsp for the single recipe and by 1tsp when doubling.",
            "datePublished": "2017-12-23T16:52:16.553Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Dan Ladouceur"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great snack I make it all the time, I have added two extra ingredients to mix in with the dry ingredients.\n\n1/2 tsp of cinnamon\n1/8 tsp Sriracha spice - it give a nice smoky flavour taste and to kick it up a notch.\neveryone just love them!!",
            "datePublished": "2019-02-20T03:08:12.570Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Yvonne Corbett"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I absolutely love this recipe. I can't stop eating them....never enough to share ...lol. I follow the recipe until the last step. After I have added the almonds to the mixture on the stove I just sprinkle the sugar/salt/cayenne pepper combination into the pan and stir to coat. Turn the heat off of the pan and once they are coated put onto baking sheet to cool. Seem sticky at first , but they have always set up for me as they cool. I have tried many other nuts and combination of nuts and they all work fine. I have also used cinnamon rather than the cayenne pepper and it is wonderful too !! I wonder if you used some kind of sugar substitute other than honey , since there is already honey in the recipe, if that would work??",
            "datePublished": "2014-01-24T12:11:19.933Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "golfmama"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Totally yummy. Perfect amount of sweet and hot. I had a variety of almonds to use up, some were roasted and salted, so I only used a teaspoon of kosher salt in the coating. I followed another reviewers advice and used a slotted spoon to transfer the nuts from the liquid to the final coating, although I didn't seem to have an excess of liquid. I stirred the nuts several times as they were cooling and I was surprised at how well the sugar/spice coating stays on the nuts. Will make again for sure!!",
            "datePublished": "2017-12-09T22:26:01.433Z"
          },
          {
